messages.content:
{
  "message": "Good catch. Airplane Mode being ON will block all cellular service, and “No SIM card detected” means the phone isn’t seeing the SIM. We’ll fix the device-side causes first, then I’ll check your line if needed.",
  "why_these_steps": [
    "Turning Airplane Mode OFF restores all radios so the phone can register on the network.",
    "A proper SIM reseat (with the phone powered off) resolves most “No SIM” detections caused by poor contact."
  ],
  "do_this_now_in_order": [
    {
      "step": "Turn OFF Airplane Mode",
      "how": "Open Quick Settings/Control Center or Settings and turn Airplane Mode OFF.",
      "what_to_check": "Wait ~30 seconds. Does the status bar change from “No Service/No SIM” to show bars or 4G/5G/LTE?"
    },
    {
      "step": "If it still says “No SIM card detected”: Reseat the SIM",
      "how": "Power the phone OFF completely. Eject the SIM tray, ensure the SIM is flat and aligned (gold contacts facing down; match the notched corner), reinsert the tray firmly, then power the phone ON.",
      "what_to_check": "After boot, give it up to 2 minutes. Do you now see signal bars or a network type? Do you see any messages like “SIM locked” or “Emergency calls only”?"
    }
  ],
  "please_reply_with": [
    "What you see after turning Airplane Mode OFF (bars/network type vs. No Service/No SIM).",
    "What you see after the SIM reseat, if needed (any changes or error messages).",
    "Your exact phone make and model (e.g., iPhone 12, Samsung Galaxy S21) so I can tailor any follow-up steps."
  ],
  "what_i_will_do_next": "If it still shows No Service or No SIM after these steps, I will check the specific line for 555-123-2002 on our side to rule out suspension or provisioning issues before we try anything else."
}

